 Context assembly\x0a Model inference\x0a Model output\x0a Output validation\x0a Intent interpretation\x0a Capability/task authorization\x0a Execution orchestration\x0a Tool/capability execution\x0a Result validation\x0a Memory/state persistence\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT bypass:\x0a\x0a- PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- Sandbox\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault access controls\x3b\x0a- task authorization\x3b\x0a- resource controls\x3b\x0a- audit requirements\x3b\x0a- security boundaries\x3b\x0a- update/recovery controls.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 5. MODEL IDENTITY\x0a\x0aEvery model implementation SHALL have a stable internal identity.\x0a\x0aModel identity SHALL be distinguishable from:\x0a\x0a- provider identity\x3b\x0a- model family\x3b\x0a- model version\x3b\x0a- deployment/runtime version\x3b\x0a- checkpoint identity\x3b\x0a- local/remote location\x3b\x0a- task role\x3b\x0a- capability set.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support recording at minimum:\x0a\x0a- internal model ID\x3b\x0a- role\x3b\x0a- provider\x3b\x0a- model family\x3b\x0a- model version\x3b\x0a- deployment identifier where applicable\x3b\x0a- capability profile\x3b\x0a- context limits\x3b\x0a- supported modalities\x3b\x0a- availability state\x3b\x0a- trust state\x3b\x0a- resource requirements\x3b\x0a- compatibility state\x3b\x0a- lifecycle state.\x0a\x0aModel identity SHALL be versioned so that historical tasks, experiments, evaluations, and audit records can identify which intelligence component produced a result.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 6. MODEL LIFECYCLE\x0a\x0aModels SHALL have explicit lifecycle states.\x0a\x0aAt minimum:\x0a\x0a- DISCOVERED\x0a- REGISTERED\x0a- VALIDATING\x0a- AVAILABLE\x0a- DEGRADED\x0a- UNAVAILABLE\x0a- QUARANTINED\x0a- RETIRED\x0a- REMOVED\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT become AVAILABLE solely because it exists on disk or is reachable through a provider.\x0a\x0aAvailability SHALL require successful validation of the requirements relevant to that model.\x0a\x0aValidation MAY include:\x0a\x0a- integrity\x3b\x0a- compatibility\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- provider availability\x3b\x0a- authentication\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- resource requirements\x3b\x0a- context support\x3b\x0a- runtime support\x3b\x0a- health\x3b\x0a- security status.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 7. MODEL REGISTRY\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL maintain a model registry or equivalent authoritative model inventory.\x0a\x0aThe registry SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- installed/local models\x3b\x0a- externally available models\x3b\x0a- temporary provider models\x3b\x0a- experimental models\x3b\x0a- candidate self-improvement models\x3b\x0a- retired models.\x0a\x0aThe registry SHALL NOT store plaintext credentials.\x0a\x0aProvider credentials SHALL remain under SecretsVault and SHALL be accessed only through authorized mechanisms.\x0a\x0aModel registry information SHALL remain portable and host-aware.\x0a\x0aHost-specific availability SHALL NOT silently alter canonical model identity.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 8. ROLE-BASED MODEL ROUTING\x0a\x0aModel selection SHALL be based on the requirements of the task rather than arbitrary model preference.\x0a\x0aRouting MAY consider:\x0a\x0a- task type\x3b\x0a- task risk\x3b\x0a- required capability\x3b\x0a- model role\x3b\x0a- reasoning requirements\x3b\x0a- context requirements\x3b\x0a- modality requirements\x3b\x0a- latency requirements\x3b\x0a- resource availability\x3b\x0a- network availability\x3b\x0a- provider availability\x3b\x0a- authorization level\x3b\x0a- task priority\x3b\x0a- reliability history\x3b\x0a- model health\x3b\x0a- model compatibility\x3b\x0a- offline requirements\x3b\x0a- cost or usage limits where applicable\x3b\x0a- creator-defined preferences.\x0a\x0aRouting SHALL NOT grant a model permissions it does not otherwise possess.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 9. S5 ORCHESTRATION\x0a\x0aS5 SHALL function as the top-level intelligence orchestration authority within LANUX's model architecture.\x0a\x0aS5 MAY:\x0a\x0a- interpret the overall objective\x3b\x0a- determine which model roles are relevant\x3b\x0a- decompose intelligence work\x3b\x0a- delegate bounded subtasks\x3b\x0a- request model collaboration\x3b\x0a- evaluate intermediate results\x3b\x0a- reconcile model outputs\x3b\x0a- request verification\x3b\x0a- select or reject proposed outputs\x3b\x0a- coordinate with Task Execution Orchestration\x3b\x0a- request approved capabilities\x3b\x0a- determine when additional reasoning is required.\x0a\x0aS5 SHALL NOT bypass:\x0a\x0a- creator authorization\x3b\x0a- PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- Sandbox\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault\x3b\x0a- resource controls\x3b\x0a- security controls\x3b\x0a- persistence rules.\x0a\x0aS5 output SHALL remain subject to the same execution governance as other model-generated intent.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 10. MODEL SPECIALIZATION\x0a\x0aSpecialized models SHALL be used according to their registered roles.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support role-specific model routing for:\x0a\x0a- discovery/research\x3b\x0a- implementation/engineering\x3b\x0a- architecture\x3b\x0a- creative generation\x3b\x0a- security\x3b\x0a- analysis\x3b\x0a- execution/piloting\x3b\x0a- memory/context support\x3b\x0a- monitoring\x3b\x0a- experimentation and challenge/testing.\x0a\x0aThe exact operational mapping of the named LANUX models SHALL remain defined by the completed LANUX architecture.\x0a\x0aA specialized model SHALL NOT be assumed to possess capabilities outside its registered profile.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 11. MODEL COLLABORATION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support multi-model collaboration where beneficial and authorized.\x0a\x0aCollaboration MAY occur through:\x0a\x0a- sequential delegation\x3b\x0a- parallel analysis\x3b\x0a- specialist review\x3b\x0a- adversarial review\x3b\x0a- verification\x3b\x0a- synthesis\x3b\x0a- escalation to S5.\x0a\x0aEach delegated model task SHALL have:\x0a\x0a- parent task identity\x3b\x0a- child task identity\x3b\x0a- defined scope\x3b\x0a- context boundary\x3b\x0a- authorization boundary\x3b\x0a- expected output\x3b\x0a- resource constraints\x3b\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- cancellation authority\x3b\x0a- result handling policy.\x0a\x0aModel collaboration SHALL NOT become an uncontrolled recursive delegation loop.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 12. DELEGATION LIMITS\x0a\x0aModel-generated delegation SHALL be bounded.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support limits for:\x0a\x0a- delegation depth\x3b\x0a- delegation count\x3b\x0a- concurrent model invocations\x3b\x0a- total model work\x3b\x0a- total context\x3b\x0a- total tokens or equivalent provider usage\x3b\x0a- execution time\x3b\x0a- resource consumption\x3b\x0a- retry count\x3b\x0a- recursion\x3b\x0a- self-triggered model chains.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT recursively create unlimited intelligence work.\x0a\x0aExceeding an orchestration limit SHALL produce a controlled failure, pause, escalation, or approval requirement according to task risk.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 13. CONTEXT ASSEMBLY\x0a\x0aBefore inference, LANUX SHALL construct an explicit model context.\x0a\x0aContext MAY include:\x0a\x0a- current task\x3b\x0a- parent task\x3b\x0a- project context\x3b\x0a- relevant memory\x3b\x0a- relevant long-term knowledge\x3b\x0a- previous execution results\x3b\x0a- approved tool outputs\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- model role\x3b\x0a- system constraints\x3b\x0a- creator instructions\x3b\x0a- relevant state\x3b\x0a- relevant diagnostics.\x0a\x0aContext SHALL be minimized to the information necessary for the task.\x0a\x0aSensitive information SHALL NOT be included merely because it exists.\x0a\x0aSecrets SHALL NOT be injected into model context unless explicitly required, authorized, and protected by the relevant security architecture.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 14. CONTEXT TRUST LEVELS\x0a\x0aContext sources SHALL be distinguishable.\x0a\x0aAt minimum:\x0a\x0a- SYSTEM\x0a- CREATOR\x0a- TRUSTED APPLICATION STATE\x0a- TASK STATE\x0a- MEMORY\x0a- TOOL OUTPUT\x0a- EXTERNAL CONTENT\x0a- MODEL-GENERATED CONTENT\x0a- UNTRUSTED CONTENT\x0a\x0aUntrusted or externally supplied content SHALL NOT be elevated to system authority merely by appearing inside model context.\x0a\x0aInstructions contained inside external content SHALL be treated as data unless explicitly authorized as instructions by the appropriate higher-trust layer.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 15. PROMPT-INJECTION RESISTANCE\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL treat model context as a security boundary.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL account for malicious or misleading instructions originating from:\x0a\x0a- websites\x3b\x0a- documents\x3b\x0a- emails\x3b\x0a- repositories\x3b\x0a- files\x3b\x0a- tool output\x3b\x0a- API responses\x3b\x0a- external services\x3b\x0a- model-generated content\x3b\x0a- memory\x3b\x0a- task artifacts.\x0a\x0aExternal instructions SHALL NOT override:\x0a\x0a- creator authority\x3b\x0a- system policy\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- security boundaries\x3b\x0a- capability restrictions\x3b\x0a- task scope.\x0a\x0aThe model SHALL never be the sole authority deciding whether an untrusted instruction is trustworthy.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 16. MODEL INPUT VALIDATION\x0a\x0aModel inputs SHALL be validated before inference.\x0a\x0aValidation MAY include:\x0a\x0a- size limits\x3b\x0a- encoding\x3b\x0a- modality compatibility\x3b\x0a- context-window limits\x3b\x0a- data classification\x3b\x0a- sensitive-data handling\x3b\x0a- prompt structure\x3b\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- source trust\x3b\x0a- attachment safety.\x0a\x0aOversized or malformed context SHALL be rejected, reduced, staged, or otherwise handled through an explicit policy.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 17. MODEL OUTPUT CONTRACT\x0a\x0aModel output SHALL have a defined schema appropriate to its purpose.\x0a\x0aWhere structured output is required, LANUX SHALL validate:\x0a\x0a- schema\x3b\x0a- types\x3b\x0a- required fields\x3b\x0a- size\x3b\x0a- identifiers\x3b\x0a- references\x3b\x0a- capability requests\x3b\x0a- task references\x3b\x0a- action parameters\x3b\x0a- confidence or uncertainty fields where supported.\x0a\x0aInvalid output SHALL NOT be executed as if valid.\x0a\x0aFree-form model text SHALL NOT automatically become executable commands.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 18. MODEL-GENERATED ACTIONS\x0a\x0aA model may propose:\x0a\x0a- a task\x3b\x0a- a subtask\x3b\x0a- a tool call\x3b\x0a- a file operation\x3b\x0a- a network operation\x3b\x0a- a code change\x3b\x0a- a browser action\x3b\x0a- a system operation\x3b\x0a- a configuration change\x3b\x0a- a self-improvement experiment\x3b\x0a- a synchronization operation.\x0a\x0aSuch proposals SHALL pass through the appropriate governance layer before execution.\x0a\x0aModel-generated intent SHALL NOT itself constitute authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 19. TOOL CALL BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aModel-generated tool calls SHALL enter through ToolRegistry and the capability governance architecture.\x0a\x0aBefore execution, LANUX SHALL validate:\x0a\x0a- tool identity\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- input schema\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- creator authorization\x3b\x0a- risk level\x3b\x0a- resource requirements\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault requirements\x3b\x0a- Sandbox requirements\x3b\x0a- audit requirements.\x0a\x0aThe model SHALL NOT directly invoke arbitrary host commands outside this boundary.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 20. PERMISSION BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aModel routing SHALL remain separate from authorization.\x0a\x0aThe following are distinct decisions:\x0a\x0a1. Which model should reason?\x0a2. What should the model reason about?\x0a3. What did the model propose?\x0a4. Is the proposed action valid?\x0a5. Is the action authorized?\x0a6. Is the action permitted now?\x0a7. Can the required resources be allocated?\x0a8. Should execution proceed?\x0a\x0aNo model selection decision SHALL implicitly answer the authorization questions.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 21. SECRETS BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aModels SHALL NOT directly manage raw SecretsVault storage.\x0a\x0aSecretsVault SHALL remain the authoritative credential storage mechanism.\x0a\x0aWhere a model requires credential-backed work:\x0a\x0aModel\x0a authorized capability\x0a permission validation\x0a SecretsVault access\x0a controlled credential use\x0a result handling\x0a\x0aRaw secret values SHALL not be persisted in:\x0a\x0a- model prompts\x3b\x0a- ordinary memory\x3b\x0a- task records\x3b\x0a- logs\x3b\x0a- audit records\x3b\x0a- model outputs\x3b\x0a- diagnostics\x3b\x0a- checkpoints\x3b\x0a- experiment records.\x0a\x0aUnless explicitly required by a separately authorized secure mechanism, secret values SHALL not be returned to the model.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 22. MODEL MEMORY INTERACTION\x0a\x0aThe MEMORY model and other intelligence components SHALL interact with memory only through governed memory mechanisms.\x0a\x0aMemory operations SHALL respect:\x0a\x0a- data classification\x3b\x0a- ownership\x3b\x0a- retention\x3b\x0a- privacy\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- creator control\x3b\x0a- synchronization rules.\x0a\x0aModel-generated memories SHALL not automatically become trusted permanent facts.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- proposed memory\x3b\x0a- validated memory\x3b\x0a- rejected memory\x3b\x0a- expired memory\x3b\x0a- corrected memory\x3b\x0a- provenance\x3b\x0a- confidence\x3b\x0a- source attribution where available.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 23. MODEL RESULT VERIFICATION\x0a\x0aImportant model outputs SHALL be independently evaluated where required.\x0a\x0aVerification MAY include:\x0a\x0a- deterministic validation\x3b\x0a- schema validation\x3b\x0a- specialist review\x3b\x0a- S5 review\x3b\x0a- execution testing\x3b\x0a- sandbox testing\x3b\x0a- source verification\x3b\x0a- cross-model comparison\x3b\x0a- regression testing\x3b\x0a- security validation\x3b\x0a- resource validation.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT verify itself by merely repeating its own output.\x0a\x0aThe required verification level SHALL depend on task risk.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 24. CONFIDENCE AND UNCERTAINTY\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- model confidence\x3b\x0a- system verification\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- execution success.\x0a\x0aA high model confidence score SHALL NOT be treated as proof of correctness.\x0a\x0aWhere meaningful, model outputs SHALL support explicit uncertainty representation.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL avoid presenting unverified model conclusions as verified facts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 25. MODEL FAILURE CLASSIFICATION\x0a\x0aModel failures SHALL be classified rather than collapsed into generic errors.\x0a\x0aAt minimum:\x0a\x0a- unavailable model\x3b\x0a- provider unavailable\x3b\x0a- authentication failure\x3b\x0a- context overflow\x3b\x0a- invalid input\x3b\x0a- invalid output\x3b\x0a- schema failure\x3b\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- resource exhaustion\x3b\x0a- rate limit\x3b\x0a- network failure\x3b\x0a- model execution failure\x3b\x0a- safety/security rejection\x3b\x0a- authorization rejection\x3b\x0a- tool execution failure\x3b\x0a- verification failure\x3b\x0a- contradictory result\x3b\x0a- degraded model state.\x0a\x0aFailure classification SHALL feed task orchestration, diagnostics, retry, fallback, and audit behavior.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 26. FALLBACK AND MODEL SUBSTITUTION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support controlled model fallback where architecture permits.\x0a\x0aFallback selection SHALL consider:\x0a\x0a- role compatibility\x3b\x0a- capability compatibility\x3b\x0a- context compatibility\x3b\x0a- task risk\x3b\x0a- resource availability\x3b\x0a- offline availability\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- model trust\x3b\x0a- expected quality requirements.\x0a\x0aFallback SHALL NOT silently change a task's required security or capability level.\x0a\x0aFor high-risk operations, fallback MAY require explicit approval or re-verification.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 27. OFFLINE INTELLIGENCE\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support offline operation to the extent the completed model architecture permits.\x0a\x0aOffline behavior SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- locally available models\x3b\x0a- unavailable remote models\x3b\x0a- queued remote-dependent work\x3b\x0a- degraded intelligence\x3b\x0a- tasks that can continue offline\x3b\x0a- tasks requiring network access.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL not falsely claim successful completion when required remote intelligence was unavailable.\x0a\x0aOffline queues SHALL respect the Network & External Services Contract.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 28. LOCAL AND REMOTE MODELS\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support both local and remote intelligence providers where applicable.\x0a\x0aLocal models MAY provide:\x0a\x0a- offline operation\x3b\x0a- lower network dependency\x3b\x0a- local data processing\x3b\x0a- predictable availability.\x0a\x0aRemote models MAY provide:\x0a\x0a- specialized capabilities\x3b\x0a- larger context\x3b\x0a- external model diversity\x3b\x0a- provider-specific functionality.\x0a\x0aNeither category shall receive unrestricted trust solely because of deployment location.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 29. MODEL PROVIDER TRUST\x0a\x0aExternal model providers SHALL be treated as external trust boundaries.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- provider identity\x3b\x0a- endpoint\x3b\x0a- authentication\x3b\x0a- transport security\x3b\x0a- service availability\x3b\x0a- provider-specific retention considerations\x3b\x0a- data transmission classification\x3b\x0a- rate limits\x3b\x0a- usage constraints\x3b\x0a- provider failure\x3b\x0a- provider substitution.\x0a\x0aSensitive information SHALL not be transmitted to external providers unless the applicable policy and authorization permit it.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 30. DATA TRANSMISSION TO MODELS\x0a\x0aBefore sending data to a remote model, LANUX SHALL determine:\x0a\x0a- what data is being transmitted\x3b\x0a- why it is required\x3b\x0a- classification\x3b\x0a- destination\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- credential context\x3b\x0a- whether redaction is possible\x3b\x0a- whether local processing is available\x3b\x0a- whether creator approval is required.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL prefer local processing where policy or sensitivity requires it.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 31. RESOURCE-AWARE INFERENCE\x0a\x0aModel execution SHALL integrate with the Resource & Capacity Management Contract.\x0a\x0aInference SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- CPU\x3b\x0a- RAM\x3b\x0a- GPU where available\x3b\x0a- storage\x3b\x0a- network\x3b\x0a- concurrency\x3b\x0a- process limits\x3b\x0a- thermal/power conditions\x3b\x0a- model size\x3b\x0a- context size\x3b\x0a- expected duration.\x0a\x0aResource pressure MAY cause:\x0a\x0a- model substitution\x3b\x0a- queueing\x3b\x0a- context reduction\x3b\x0a- concurrency reduction\x3b\x0a- background-task suspension\x3b\x0a- lower-priority work deferral\x3b\x0a- controlled failure.\x0a\x0aResource management SHALL not silently terminate unrelated host processes.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 32. INTERACTIVE VS BACKGROUND INTELLIGENCE\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL distinguish interactive intelligence from background intelligence.\x0a\x0aInteractive work MAY receive higher responsiveness priority.\x0a\x0aBackground work MAY include:\x0a\x0a- monitoring\x3b\x0a- diagnostics\x3b\x0a- self-improvement\x3b\x0a- indexing\x3b\x0a- evaluation\x3b\x0a- synchronization\x3b\x0a- maintenance.\x0a\x0aBackground intelligence SHALL remain bounded by resource budgets and permissions.\x0a\x0aInteractive requests SHALL not automatically starve critical background safety operations.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 33. MODEL SCHEDULING\x0a\x0aModel invocations SHALL be schedulable.\x0a\x0aScheduling MAY consider:\x0a\x0a- priority\x3b\x0a- task dependencies\x3b\x0a- model availability\x3b\x0a- resource availability\x3b\x0a- deadline\x3b\x0a- latency\x3b\x0a- risk\x3b\x0a- creator interaction\x3b\x0a- queue state\x3b\x0a- fairness.\x0a\x0aThe scheduler SHALL prevent uncontrolled model invocation storms.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 34. CONCURRENCY\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL define concurrency limits for:\x0a\x0a- total model calls\x3b\x0a- per-model calls\x3b\x0a- per-task calls\x3b\x0a- per-provider calls\x3b\x0a- background intelligence\x3b\x0a- self-improvement\x3b\x0a- evaluation.\x0a\x0aConcurrency limits SHALL be integrated with Resource & Capacity Management and Task Execution Orchestration.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 35. RETRIES\x0a\x0aModel retries SHALL be controlled.\x0a\x0aRetry policy SHALL consider:\x0a\x0a- failure class\x3b\x0a- task idempotency\x3b\x0a- model state\x3b\x0a- provider state\x3b\x0a- resource availability\x3b\x0a- retry budget\x3b\x0a- task risk\x3b\x0a- context stability.\x0a\x0aRepeated identical failures SHALL trigger escalation, fallback, pause, or termination rather than uncontrolled retry loops.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 36. MODEL OUTPUT CACHING\x0a\x0aCaching MAY be used where safe.\x0a\x0aCached intelligence SHALL include sufficient identity and context metadata to prevent stale or cross-task reuse.\x0a\x0aSensitive model outputs SHALL not be cached outside their authorized storage domain.\x0a\x0aCached results SHALL not bypass current authorization or verification requirements.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 37. MODEL VERSIONING\x0a\x0aModel outputs SHALL remain attributable to the exact model version or deployment identity available at execution time.\x0a\x0aChanges to:\x0a\x0a- model weights\x3b\x0a- model provider\x3b\x0a- inference engine\x3b\x0a- system prompts\x3b\x0a- orchestration policy\x3b\x0a- context assembly\x3b\x0a- tool schemas\x3b\x0a- safety policy\x0a\x0aMAY change model behavior and SHALL therefore be represented in the relevant version/state metadata.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 38. MODEL EVALUATION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL maintain controlled model evaluation mechanisms.\x0a\x0aEvaluation MAY assess:\x0a\x0a- correctness\x3b\x0a- reliability\x3b\x0a- task completion\x3b\x0a- tool-use accuracy\x3b\x0a- security behavior\x3b\x0a- refusal behavior where applicable\x3b\x0a- resource usage\x3b\x0a- latency\x3b\x0a- consistency\x3b\x0a- regression behavior\x3b\x0a- offline behavior.\x0a\x0aEvaluation results SHALL NOT automatically promote a model to production use.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 39. EXPERIMENTAL MODELS\x0a\x0aExperimental models SHALL remain isolated from production intelligence until approved.\x0a\x0aExperimental model state SHALL be distinguishable from:\x0a\x0a- production models\x3b\x0a- candidate models\x3b\x0a- retired models.\x0a\x0aExperiments SHALL use the Self-Improvement & Growth Contract and SHALL maintain:\x0a\x0a- experiment identity\x3b\x0a- model identity\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- dataset/context provenance where applicable\x3b\x0a- evaluation criteria\x3b\x0a- results\x3b\x0a- resource usage\x3b\x0a- failure state\x3b\x0a- promotion decision.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 40. SELF-IMPROVEMENT MODEL PROMOTION\x0a\x0aA model candidate SHALL pass the defined self-improvement lifecycle before promotion.\x0a\x0aPromotion SHALL require, as applicable:\x0a\x0a- candidate validation\x3b\x0a- evaluation\x3b\x0a- security verification\x3b\x0a- regression testing\x3b\x0a- compatibility verification\x3b\x0a- resource verification\x3b\x0a- rollback readiness\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- controlled activation.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT promote itself directly into production.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 41. MODEL CHECKPOINTS\x0a\x0aWhere model checkpoints are used, LANUX SHALL track:\x0a\x0a- checkpoint identity\x3b\x0a- source model\x3b\x0a- creation context\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- integrity\x3b\x0a- storage location\x3b\x0a- ownership\x3b\x0a- compatibility\x3b\x0a- evaluation state\x3b\x0a- promotion state.\x0a\x0aCheckpoint data SHALL follow persistence, backup, recovery, and data-handling rules.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 42. MODEL CONTEXT CHECKPOINTS\x0a\x0aLong-running intelligence work MAY checkpoint:\x0a\x0a- task state\x3b\x0a- model state where supported\x3b\x0a- context references\x3b\x0a- intermediate results\x3b\x0a- delegated subtasks\x3b\x0a- pending decisions\x3b\x0a- verification state.\x0a\x0aCheckpoints SHALL avoid embedding secrets unnecessarily.\x0a\x0aCheckpoint restoration SHALL validate compatibility and integrity before resuming.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 43. MODEL STATE AND USB PORTABILITY\x0a\x0aPortable LANUX SHALL not assume that model state is tied to a fixed Windows machine.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- portable model assets\x3b\x0a- host-specific acceleration/runtime state\x3b\x0a- provider configuration\x3b\x0a- task state\x3b\x0a- cache\x3b\x0a- temporary inference artifacts.\x0a\x0aUSB removal SHALL stop or checkpoint active model work according to the USB Removal & Safe Shutdown Contract.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 44. MODEL STATE AND HOST CHANGES\x0a\x0aWhen LANUX moves between hosts, model availability SHALL be re-evaluated.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL not assume:\x0a\x0a- identical CPU\x3b\x0a- identical RAM\x3b\x0a- identical GPU\x3b\x0a- identical drivers\x3b\x0a- identical runtime\x3b\x0a- identical network\x3b\x0a- identical external providers\x3b\x0a- identical permissions.\x0a\x0aThe model registry SHALL represent host-specific availability separately from canonical model identity.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 45. MODEL HEALTH\x0a\x0aModel health SHALL be integrated with Health & Diagnostics.\x0a\x0aHealth SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- available\x3b\x0a- degraded\x3b\x0a- unavailable\x3b\x0a- failed\x3b\x0a- unknown\x3b\x0a- not applicable.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL not be marked healthy solely because its process starts.\x0a\x0aHealth MAY include evidence from:\x0a\x0a- initialization\x3b\x0a- inference\x3b\x0a- schema validation\x3b\x0a- latency\x3b\x0a- error rate\x3b\x0a- resource behavior\x3b\x0a- provider connectivity\x3b\x0a- integrity validation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 46. MODEL SECURITY STATE\x0a\x0aA model MAY enter a security-restricted state because of:\x0a\x0a- integrity failure\x3b\x0a- unexpected modification\x3b\x0a- untrusted source\x3b\x0a- incompatible update\x3b\x0a- suspicious behavior\x3b\x0a- policy violation\x3b\x0a- unauthorized configuration\x3b\x0a- compromised provider\x3b\x0a- invalid checkpoint.\x0a\x0aA restricted model SHALL not execute privileged or sensitive work until cleared by the appropriate security process.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 47. MODEL-TO-MODEL TRUST\x0a\x0aOne model's output SHALL be treated as model-generated content when passed to another model.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT elevate another model's output to system authority merely because it came from S5 or another trusted role.\x0a\x0aCross-model communication SHALL preserve:\x0a\x0a- provenance\x3b\x0a- task identity\x3b\x0a- source model identity\x3b\x0a- trust classification\x3b\x0a- authorization context.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 48. ADVERSARIAL REVIEW\x0a\x0aWhere required by task risk, LANUX SHALL support adversarial model review.\x0a\x0aA reviewer model MAY attempt to identify:\x0a\x0a- incorrect assumptions\x3b\x0a- missing requirements\x3b\x0a- security issues\x3b\x0a- unsupported conclusions\x3b\x0a- tool misuse\x3b\x0a- edge cases\x3b\x0a- contradictions\x3b\x0a- regressions.\x0a\x0aAdversarial review SHALL remain advisory until its findings pass the appropriate decision and authorization process.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 49. CONTRADICTORY MODEL OUTPUTS\x0a\x0aWhen models disagree, LANUX SHALL preserve the disagreement rather than silently collapsing it into an arbitrary answer.\x0a\x0aResolution MAY use:\x0a\x0a- evidence\x3b\x0a- deterministic validation\x3b\x0a- specialist review\x3b\x0a- S5 synthesis\x3b\x0a- creator input\x3b\x0a- task-specific policy.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- disagreement\x3b\x0a- uncertainty\x3b\x0a- verified resolution.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 50. CREATOR INTERVENTION\x0a\x0aCreator intervention SHALL remain available for decisions that require human judgment.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- approval\x3b\x0a- rejection\x3b\x0a- clarification\x3b\x0a- cancellation\x3b\x0a- model selection override where permitted\x3b\x0a- task pause\x3b\x0a- task termination\x3b\x0a- security escalation.\x0a\x0aCreator intervention SHALL not be silently replaced by model consensus.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 51. MODEL AUTONOMY\x0a\x0aAutonomous model behavior SHALL remain bounded by:\x0a\x0a- creator authorization\x3b\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- capability permissions\x3b\x0a- resource budgets\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- network policy\x3b\x0a- persistence policy\x3b\x0a- time limits\x3b\x0a- delegation limits.\x0a\x0aAutonomy SHALL mean authorized execution within defined boundaries, not unrestricted authority.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 52. MODEL CONTEXT PRIVACY\x0a\x0aModel context SHALL follow the Data Handling & Privacy Contract.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL minimize:\x0a\x0a- personal data\x3b\x0a- host information\x3b\x0a- credentials\x3b\x0a- unrelated project information\x3b\x0a- unrelated memory\x3b\x0a- unnecessary file contents\x3b\x0a- unrelated task history.\x0a\x0aContext construction SHALL be purpose-limited.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 53. MODEL LOGGING\x0a\x0aModel orchestration SHALL generate operational and security-relevant records sufficient to reconstruct important decisions.\x0a\x0aLogs SHALL identify where appropriate:\x0a\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- model\x3b\x0a- model version\x3b\x0a- invocation\x3b\x0a- provider\x3b\x0a- outcome\x3b\x0a- duration\x3b\x0a- failure\x3b\x0a- fallback\x3b\x0a- verification\x3b\x0a- authorization decision\x3b\x0a- resource event.\x0a\x0aLogs SHALL not contain raw secrets or unnecessary sensitive model context.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 54. MODEL AUDIT\x0a\x0aSecurity-sensitive model events SHALL be auditable.\x0a\x0aAudit records MAY include:\x0a\x0a- model activation\x3b\x0a- model registration\x3b\x0a- model quarantine\x3b\x0a- model promotion\x3b\x0a- model retirement\x3b\x0a- privileged model request\x3b\x0a- rejected tool call\x3b\x0a- authorization decision\x3b\x0a- fallback\x3b\x0a- security rejection\x3b\x0a- model-generated configuration proposal\x3b\x0a- self-improvement promotion.\x0a\x0aAudit records SHALL preserve enough provenance to distinguish model intent from actual system action.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 55. PERSISTENCE\x0a\x0aModel orchestration state SHALL integrate with the Persistence & State Contract.\x0a\x0aPersistent model state MAY include:\x0a\x0a- registry\x3b\x0a- version metadata\x3b\x0a- routing configuration\x3b\x0a- evaluation results\x3b\x0a- task references\x3b\x0a- checkpoints\x3b\x0a- approved candidates\x3b\x0a- health history\x3b\x0a- provider metadata.\x0a\x0aTransient inference state SHALL remain temporary unless explicitly checkpointed.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 56. CRASH RECOVERY\x0a\x0aAfter a crash, LANUX SHALL reconstruct model orchestration state from durable records.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- completed inference\x3b\x0a- unknown inference\x3b\x0a- interrupted inference\x3b\x0a- partial output\x3b\x0a- failed invocation\x3b\x0a- pending retry\x3b\x0a- invalidated context.\x0a\x0aUnknown execution SHALL not be falsely recorded as successful.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 57. USB REMOVAL\x0a\x0aWhen USB removal is detected:\x0a\x0a- new model work SHALL stop\x3b\x0a- active model tasks SHALL enter safe shutdown/checkpoint behavior\x3b\x0a- temporary inference state SHALL be handled according to persistence policy\x3b\x0a- remote requests SHALL not be assumed complete\x3b\x0a- model state SHALL be persisted only where safe\x3b\x0a- recovery metadata SHALL be written where possible.\x0a\x0aNo model SHALL continue using LANUX-owned resources after confirmed USB removal unless explicitly designed and authorized by the runtime architecture.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 58. UPDATE INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aModel updates SHALL follow the Update & Upgrade Contract.\x0a\x0aUpdates SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- source verification\x3b\x0a- compatibility checks\x3b\x0a- backup\x3b\x0a- staging\x3b\x0a- integrity verification\x3b\x0a- controlled activation\x3b\x0a- health validation\x3b\x0a- rollback.\x0a\x0aA failed model update SHALL not leave the production registry falsely reporting the new model as healthy.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 59. RECOVERY INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aModel recovery SHALL follow the Recovery & Disaster Recovery Contract.\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL preserve:\x0a\x0a- model identity\x3b\x0a- authorization boundaries\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- provenance\x3b\x0a- compatibility\x3b\x0a- rollback information.\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL not restore an untrusted or invalid model merely because it is present in a backup.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 60. NETWORK INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aRemote model orchestration SHALL follow the Network & External Services Contract.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- model unavailable because network is offline\x3b\x0a- provider unavailable\x3b\x0a- authentication failure\x3b\x0a- rate limiting\x3b\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- remote execution failure.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL not misclassify network failure as model correctness or task completion.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 61. RESOURCE PRESSURE\x0a\x0aWhen resources become constrained, LANUX MAY:\x0a\x0a- defer background inference\x3b\x0a- reduce concurrency\x3b\x0a- select a lighter compatible model\x3b\x0a- pause self-improvement\x3b\x0a- shorten noncritical context\x3b\x0a- queue work\x3b\x0a- cancel low-priority work.\x0a\x0aCritical safety and shutdown operations SHALL retain required resources where possible.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 62. COST AND USAGE GOVERNANCE\x0a\x0aWhere external models incur usage or monetary cost, model invocation SHALL be governed by:\x0a\x0a- configured limits\x3b\x0a- task priority\x3b\x0a- creator policy\x3b\x0a- provider limits\x3b\x0a- budget state\x3b\x0a- retry controls.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL not autonomously bypass configured provider or cost limits.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 63. MODEL DISCOVERY\x0a\x0aModel discovery MAY identify:\x0a\x0a- newly installed local models\x3b\x0a- newly available providers\x3b\x0a- updated model versions\x3b\x0a- experimental candidates\x3b\x0a- restored models\x3b\x0a- missing expected models.\x0a\x0aDiscovery SHALL NOT automatically activate newly discovered intelligence for privileged work.\x0a\x0aNew models SHALL pass registration and validation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 64. MODEL REMOVAL\x0a\x0aModel removal SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- active tasks\x3b\x0a- dependencies\x3b\x0a- checkpoints\x3b\x0a- evaluation records\x3b\x0a- routing references\x3b\x0a- rollback capability\x3b\x0a- persistent state\x3b\x0a- update history.\x0a\x0aRemoving a model SHALL not silently remove unrelated user data or host software.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 65. MODEL INVENTORY\x0a\x0aDiagnostics SHALL be able to report an inventory including, where available:\x0a\x0a- model identity\x3b\x0a- role\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- source\x3b\x0a- lifecycle state\x3b\x0a- health\x3b\x0a- resource requirements\x3b\x0a- availability\x3b\x0a- security state\x3b\x0a- evaluation state\x3b\x0a- routing eligibility.\x0a\x0aInventory output SHALL respect privacy and authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 66. MODEL ROUTING DECISION RECORD\x0a\x0aImportant routing decisions SHALL be reconstructable.\x0a\x0aWhere applicable, the system SHALL retain:\x0a\x0a- task identity\x3b\x0a- requested role\x3b\x0a- candidate models\x3b\x0a- eligibility results\x3b\x0a- selected model\x3b\x0a- reason/category for selection\x3b\x0a- fallback\x3b\x0a- constraints\x3b\x0a- authorization state.\x0a\x0aSensitive internal reasoning or raw prompts SHALL not be persisted merely for audit purposes unless explicitly required and authorized.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 67. DETERMINISTIC REQUIREMENTS\x0a\x0aWhere a task requires deterministic behavior, LANUX SHALL prefer deterministic mechanisms over unconstrained model generation.\x0a\x0aExamples MAY include:\x0a\x0a- schema validation\x3b\x0a- exact configuration validation\x3b\x0a- cryptographic verification\x3b\x0a- filesystem ownership checks\x3b\x0a- state-machine transitions\x3b\x0a- permission checks\x3b\x0a- integrity checks.\x0a\x0aModels SHALL assist deterministic systems rather than replace them.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 68. MODEL OUTPUT VS SYSTEM FACT\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL maintain a clear distinction between:\x0a\x0a- model claim\x3b\x0a- model recommendation\x3b\x0a- model proposal\x3b\x0a- verified system fact\x3b\x0a- executed system result.\x0a\x0aThe UI, logs, memory, and persistence layers SHALL avoid presenting unverified model claims as system facts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 69. MODEL-DRIVEN MEMORY CREATION\x0a\x0aWhen a model proposes durable memory, LANUX SHALL determine:\x0a\x0a- source\x3b\x0a- purpose\x3b\x0a- sensitivity\x3b\x0a- validity\x3b\x0a- retention\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- authorization.\x0a\x0aDurable memory SHALL not be created solely because a model stated something confidently.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 70. MODEL-DRIVEN SELF-IMPROVEMENT\x0a\x0aModels MAY identify improvement opportunities, generate proposals, design experiments, analyze results, or challenge existing behavior.\x0a\x0aHowever:\x0a\x0a- proposals are not promotions\x3b\x0a- experiments are not production\x3b\x0a- candidate models are not trusted models\x3b\x0a- evaluation is not authorization\x3b\x0a- model consensus is not creator approval.\x0a\x0aAll self-improvement SHALL follow the Self-Improvement & Growth Contract.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 71. MODEL-DRIVEN CONFIGURATION CHANGES\x0a\x0aA model may propose configuration changes.\x0a\x0aSuch changes SHALL pass through:\x0a\x0a- configuration validation\x3b\x0a- ownership rules\x3b\x0a- permission checks\x3b\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- update/recovery rules\x3b\x0a- creator authorization where required.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL NOT directly modify protected configuration merely because it generated a valid configuration file.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 72. MODEL-DRIVEN REPOSITORY CHANGES\x0a\x0aWhere LANUX is authorized to modify its repository, model-generated changes SHALL remain governed by:\x0a\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- repository boundaries\x3b\x0a- ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- Sandbox\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- testing\x3b\x0a- verification\x3b\x0a- persistence\x3b\x0a- audit\x3b\x0a- canonical synchronization.\x0a\x0aThe model SHALL not treat generated code as automatically trusted.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 73. MODEL-DRIVEN HOST ACTIONS\x0a\x0aModel proposals affecting the host SHALL be subject to the Host Installation, Tool Capability Governance, Security Boundary, and Permission architectures.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL never interpret a model-generated host action as implicit creator authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 74. MODEL CONTEXT INJECTION FROM TOOLS\x0a\x0aTool output SHALL retain its trust classification.\x0a\x0aTool output that contains instructions SHALL not automatically become executable model instructions.\x0a\x0aThe orchestration layer SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- tool result data\x3b\x0a- tool metadata\x3b\x0a- tool error\x3b\x0a- tool-generated recommendation\x3b\x0a- external content returned by tool.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 75. EXTERNAL MODEL CONTENT\x0a\x0aExternal model responses SHALL be treated as externally generated content unless the model is explicitly registered as a trusted internal component.\x0a\x0aExternal model instructions SHALL not override LANUX's internal governance.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 76. MODEL PROVIDER FAILOVER\x0a\x0aProvider failover SHALL preserve task identity and authorization.\x0a\x0aChanging providers SHALL not silently:\x0a\x0a- expand data access\x3b\x0a- change task scope\x3b\x0a- expose secrets\x3b\x0a- change security classification\x3b\x0a- bypass verification\x3b\x0a- remove creator approval requirements.\x0a\x0aProvider changes SHALL be observable where relevant.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 77. MODEL ROUTING CONFIGURATION\x0a\x0aRouting policies SHALL be configurable without hard-coding vendor assumptions.\x0a\x0aConfiguration SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- preferred role\x3b\x0a- fallback role\x3b\x0a- availability rules\x3b\x0a- resource thresholds\x3b\x0a- offline behavior\x3b\x0a- risk thresholds\x3b\x0a- concurrency limits\x3b\x0a- provider restrictions\x3b\x0a- creator-defined preferences.\x0a\x0aConfiguration changes SHALL follow the Configuration & Environment Contract.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 78. MODEL COMPATIBILITY\x0a\x0aA model SHALL be considered compatible only when required:\x0a\x0a- runtime\x3b\x0a- architecture\x3b\x0a- hardware\x3b\x0a- context\x3b\x0a- tokenizer/input\x3b\x0a- output format\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- provider\x3b\x0a- configuration\x0a\x0arequirements are satisfied.\x0a\x0aCompatibility SHALL be re-evaluated after:\x0a\x0a- host changes\x3b\x0a- runtime changes\x3b\x0a- model updates\x3b\x0a- configuration changes\x3b\x0a- dependency updates.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 79. MODEL STARTUP\x0a\x0aModel initialization SHALL be explicit and observable.\x0a\x0aStartup SHALL verify:\x0a\x0a- identity\x3b\x0a- source\x3b\x0a- integrity\x3b\x0a- compatibility\x3b\x0a- required runtime\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- credentials if required\x3b\x0a- resource availability\x3b\x0a- security state.\x0a\x0aInitialization failure SHALL prevent false readiness.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 80. MODEL SHUTDOWN\x0a\x0aModel shutdown SHALL:\x0a\x0a- stop new inference\x3b\x0a- allow safe completion where permitted\x3b\x0a- checkpoint where appropriate\x3b\x0a- terminate managed processes\x3b\x0a- release resources\x3b\x0a- flush relevant state\x3b\x0a- record outcome.\x0a\x0aForced termination SHALL be distinguishable from graceful shutdown.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 81. MODEL LOCKING\x0a\x0aWhere model state can be concurrently modified, LANUX SHALL use appropriate ownership and locking.\x0a\x0aThis SHALL prevent:\x0a\x0a- simultaneous incompatible updates\x3b\x0a- checkpoint corruption\x3b\x0a- registry races\x3b\x0a- duplicate activation\x3b\x0a- conflicting promotion\x3b\x0a- concurrent destructive operations.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 82. MODEL-RELATED TEMPORARY DATA\x0a\x0aTemporary inference artifacts SHALL be:\x0a\x0a- scoped\x3b\x0a- owned\x3b\x0a- isolated\x3b\x0a- cleaned up\x3b\x0a- excluded from canonical synchronization unless explicitly classified otherwise.\x0a\x0aCleanup SHALL not delete files outside LANUX ownership.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 83. MODEL STORAGE PRESSURE\x0a\x0aModel assets, checkpoints, caches, and evaluation artifacts SHALL participate in Resource & Capacity Management.\x0a\x0aCleanup SHALL respect:\x0a\x0a- retention policy\x3b\x0a- model importance\x3b\x0a- rollback requirements\x3b\x0a- recovery requirements\x3b\x0a- ownership\x3b\x0a- active task state.\x0a\x0aA storage-pressure response SHALL not delete the only valid recovery copy without explicit policy allowing it.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 84. MODEL TELEMETRY\x0a\x0aTelemetry SHALL be limited to what is necessary for:\x0a\x0a- diagnostics\x3b\x0a- reliability\x3b\x0a- resource management\x3b\x0a- security\x3b\x0a- evaluation\x3b\x0a- creator-visible operational status.\x0a\x0aTelemetry SHALL respect privacy and data-handling rules.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 85. MODEL PERFORMANCE HISTORY\x0a\x0aLANUX MAY maintain historical model performance information.\x0a\x0aSuch information SHALL be contextualized by:\x0a\x0a- task type\x3b\x0a- model version\x3b\x0a- environment\x3b\x0a- resource state\x3b\x0a- provider\x3b\x0a- evaluation methodology.\x0a\x0aHistorical performance SHALL not be treated as permanent truth after model or environment changes.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 86. MODEL SELECTION UNDER DEGRADED CONDITIONS\x0a\x0aIf no ideal model is available, LANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- safe degraded continuation\x3b\x0a- queued continuation\x3b\x0a- creator decision required\x3b\x0a- task impossible under current conditions.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL not silently lower safety requirements to obtain completion.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 87. MODEL SELECTION UNDER SECURITY HOLD\x0a\x0aWhen LANUX is under security hold:\x0a\x0a- privileged model actions SHALL stop\x3b\x0a- sensitive context SHALL be restricted\x3b\x0a- model-generated host changes SHALL stop\x3b\x0a- external transmission MAY be restricted\x3b\x0a- recovery/diagnostic intelligence MAY continue only within the security policy.\x0a\x0aSecurity hold SHALL take precedence over ordinary task convenience.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 88. MODEL SELECTION UNDER RECOVERY\x0a\x0aDuring recovery, intelligence use SHALL be limited to what is necessary and authorized.\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL prefer deterministic validation and known-good mechanisms over untrusted model reasoning for critical state reconstruction.\x0a\x0aModels MAY assist recovery analysis but SHALL not override recovery integrity checks.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 89. MODEL SELECTION DURING UPDATE\x0a\x0aDuring updates, models SHALL not modify the update transaction outside authorized update mechanisms.\x0a\x0aThe active model SHALL respect update locks and transaction boundaries.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 90. MODEL ORCHESTRATION STATE MACHINE\x0a\x0aAt minimum, model invocation SHALL conceptually transition through:\x0a\x0aREQUESTED\x0a ELIGIBILITY_CHECK\x0a CONTEXT_ASSEMBLY\x0a AUTHORIZATION_CHECK\x0a RESOURCE_CHECK\x0a QUEUED\x0a INITIALIZING\x0a INVOKING\x0a OUTPUT_VALIDATION\x0a RESULT_VERIFICATION\x0a ACCEPTED / REJECTED / FAILED\x0a PERSISTED\x0a COMPLETED\x0a\x0aFallback, cancellation, timeout, interruption, recovery, and security hold states SHALL be represented explicitly.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 91. FALSE SUCCESS PREVENTION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL never mark model work successful merely because:\x0a\x0a- a provider returned HTTP success\x3b\x0a- a process started\x3b\x0a- a response was non-empty\x3b\x0a- the model claimed success\x3b\x0a- a tool call was generated.\x0a\x0aCompletion SHALL require the applicable result verification.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 92. MODEL-DRIVEN TASK CREATION\x0a\x0aWhen a model creates a task, the new task SHALL enter Task Execution Orchestration through the normal task lifecycle.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL receive:\x0a\x0a- task identity\x3b\x0a- parent identity\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- requirements\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- priority\x3b\x0a- resource constraints\x3b\x0a- lifecycle state.\x0a\x0aThe model SHALL not directly create unbounded executable work outside the task system.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 93. MODEL-DRIVEN CAPABILITY DISCOVERY\x0a\x0aModels MAY request capability discovery.\x0a\x0aDiscovery SHALL not itself grant permission.\x0a\x0aThe Tool Capability Governance Contract SHALL determine:\x0a\x0a- available capability\x3b\x0a- eligibility\x3b\x0a- risk\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- execution constraints.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 94. MODEL OUTPUT SANITIZATION\x0a\x0aOutputs entering other subsystems SHALL be sanitized according to destination.\x0a\x0aDifferent validation SHALL apply to:\x0a\x0a- UI text\x3b\x0a- memory\x3b\x0a- task definitions\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- filesystem paths\x3b\x0a- shell commands\x3b\x0a- URLs\x3b\x0a- API requests\x3b\x0a- code\x3b\x0a- repository changes.\x0a\x0aNo universal "safe model output" assumption SHALL exist.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 95. MODEL OUTPUT TO UI\x0a\x0aUI presentation SHALL distinguish where appropriate:\x0a\x0a- model-generated response\x3b\x0a- verified system status\x3b\x0a- task progress\x3b\x0a- tool result\x3b\x0a- warning\x3b\x0a- uncertainty\x3b\x0a- failure.\x0a\x0aThe UI SHALL not imply that model claims are verified system facts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 96. MODEL ORCHESTRATION WITH COMMUNICATION\x0a\x0aLANUX's communication layer SHALL receive structured orchestration state where necessary.\x0a\x0aThe system SHALL support visibility into:\x0a\x0a- active model\x3b\x0a- delegated model\x3b\x0a- task progress\x3b\x0a- waiting state\x3b\x0a- approval requirement\x3b\x0a- fallback\x3b\x0a- failure\x3b\x0a- completion.\x0a\x0aInternal sensitive context SHALL remain protected.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 97. MODEL ORCHESTRATION WITH RESOURCE GOVERNANCE\x0a\x0aResource limits SHALL be enforceable independently of model intent.\x0a\x0aA model request exceeding resource policy SHALL be rejected, queued, reduced, or escalated.\x0a\x0aModel-generated requests SHALL not be able to increase their own resource budgets without the applicable authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 98. MODEL ORCHESTRATION WITH SECURITY GOVERNANCE\x0a\x0aSecurity controls SHALL operate independently of model reasoning.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL not be permitted to:\x0a\x0a- disable security controls\x3b\x0a- disable audit\x3b\x0a- bypass permissions\x3b\x0a- reveal protected credentials\x3b\x0a- modify security policy without authorization\x3b\x0a- disable recovery protections\x3b\x0a- remove its own restrictions.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 99. MODEL ORCHESTRATION WITH CANONICAL STATE\x0a\x0aModel configuration and model metadata SHALL follow canonical state rules.\x0a\x0aCanonical synchronization SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- portable model metadata\x3b\x0a- canonical configuration\x3b\x0a- host-specific availability\x3b\x0a- local caches\x3b\x0a- credentials\x3b\x0a- temporary inference state\x3b\x0a- experimental state.\x0a\x0aSecrets SHALL never enter GitHub canonical state as plaintext.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 100. REQUIREMENTS GATE\x0a\x0aBefore implementation of the production model orchestration layer, LANUX SHALL establish from the completed LANUX system:\x0a\x0a- exact model inventory\x3b\x0a- final model roles\x3b\x0a- actual provider requirements\x3b\x0a- actual local model requirements\x3b\x0a- inference runtime requirements\x3b\x0a- hardware requirements\x3b\x0a- context limits\x3b\x0a- supported modalities\x3b\x0a- routing rules\x3b\x0a- fallback rules\x3b\x0a- evaluation mechanisms\x3b\x0a- model storage requirements\x3b\x0a- model update mechanisms\x3b\x0a- offline requirements\x3b\x0a- actual S5 implementation boundaries\x3b\x0a- existing model interfaces\x3b\x0a- existing model orchestration components\x3b\x0a- existing tests\x3b\x0a- actual self-improvement integration points.\x0a\x0aImplementation SHALL begin only after these requirements are verified against the LANUX repository and existing architecture.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 101. IMPLEMENTATION BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aThis contract authorizes architecture definition only.\x0a\x0aIt does NOT authorize:\x0a\x0a- modifying the LANUX development repository\x3b\x0a- changing model implementations\x3b\x0a- installing model runtimes\x3b\x0a- downloading model weights\x3b\x0a- changing providers\x3b\x0a- changing prompts\x3b\x0a- changing ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- changing PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- changing SecretsVault\x3b\x0a- changing TaskStore\x3b\x0a- changing ProjectStore\x3b\x0a- changing self-improvement code\x3b\x0a- changing host configuration\x3b\x0a- changing USB runtime files\x3b\x0a- changing NUKRAX web files\x3b\x0a- changing recovery media.\x0a\x0aAny future implementation SHALL first inspect the completed LANUX architecture and existing tests.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 102. SOURCE OF TRUTH\x0a\x0aThe completed LANUX repository remains authoritative for actual implementation details.\x0a\x0aThis contract defines the portable USB architecture boundary and intended requirements.\x0a\x0aWhere this contract and the completed implementation differ, the discrepancy SHALL be explicitly identified and resolved before production implementation.\x0a\x0aNo silent architectural assumption SHALL be converted into implementation behavior.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 103. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CONDITION\x0a\x0aThe Model & Intelligence Orchestration architecture SHALL be considered complete only when:\x0a\x0a- every production model has an explicit identity and role\x3b\x0a- model routing is governed\x3b\x0a- model input and output boundaries are defined\x3b\x0a- model-generated actions pass through authorization\x3b\x0a- S5 orchestration remains bounded\x3b\x0a- specialized model delegation is controlled\x3b\x0a- context trust is explicit\x3b\x0a- secrets remain separated\x3b\x0a- memory interaction is governed\x3b\x0a- resource usage is controlled\x3b\x0a- local and remote models are distinguishable\x3b\x0a- offline behavior is defined\x3b\x0a- fallback is controlled\x3b\x0a- model health is observable\x3b\x0a- model updates and recovery are governed\x3b\x0a- self-improvement promotion is controlled\x3b\x0a- model provenance is preserved\x3b\x0a- audit and logging requirements are defined\x3b\x0a- false success is prevented\x3b\x0a- security holds are respected\x3b\x0a- USB portability is preserved\x3b\x0a- host changes are handled\x3b\x0a- implementation requirements are verified from the completed LANUX system.\x0a\x0aUntil these conditions are satisfied, the production model orchestration implementation SHALL remain gated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 104. STATUS\x0a\x0aContract: MODEL & INTELLIGENCE ORCHESTRATION CONTRACT\x0aNumber: 20\x0aStatus: LOCKED\x0aImplementation: NOT AUTHORIZED BY THIS CONTRACT\x0aProduction implementation: GATED ON FINAL LANUX REQUIREMENTS\x0a'@ | Set-Content -LiteralPath $Path -Encoding UTF8\x0a\x0aGet-Item -LiteralPath $Path | Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime;ef5fddfa-7684-4d37-8220-d78e4665db4e
